evidence.quote:
Let $p:Y\to T$ be projective, where $T$ is a smooth complex algebraic curve and $Y$ is a reduced, pure three-dimensional, Cohen–Macaulay Du Bois variety. Components of $Y$ may map to points. On an open $T^\circ$ with flat Du Bois surface fibers, coherent base change and topological local systems, put $\mathbb H=R^1p_*\mathbb Q$. Then $$\label{nu2:eq:hodge}
 R^1p_*\omega_Y=\omega_T\otimes F^0\mathcal H^\vee,
 \qquad F^1\mathcal H^\vee=0
 \quad\text{on }T^\circ,$$ where $\mathcal H=\mathbb H\otimes\mathcal O$. For a flat local section $\alpha$ of $\mathbb H\otimes\mathbb C$, let $\alpha_{\mathcal O}$ be its image in $R^1p_*\mathcal O_Y$. The functional corresponding to $s$ is $\alpha\mapsto\langle s/dt,\alpha_{\mathcal O}\rangle$ under relative Serre duality. The variation is graded-polarizable and admissible, with quasi-unipotent monodromy. A section $s$ extending over a missing parameter obeys $$\label{nu2:eq:one-pole}
 \langle s/dt,\alpha_{\mathcal O}\rangle
 =O\bigl(|t|^{-1}(1+|\log|t||)^N\bigr)$$ in flat frames on bounded-argument sectors. Moreover, a germ of $R^1p_*\omega_Y$ supported at a point is zero if its absolute trace annihilates the image of $H_c^2(Y_U,\mathbb C)\to H_c^2(Y_U,\mathcal O_Y)$ on one sufficiently small disk $U$. Neither assertion at the missing point requires a flat map or a reduced Du Bois central fiber.

outputs.exact_statement:
The tautological form and its residue satisfy $$\label{bl:cov:residue-form}
 \sigma\in H^0\bigl(Y,\omega_Y(R+D_h)\bigr),\qquad
 \Omega=\mathop{\mathrm{res}}_R(\sigma)\in H^0\bigl(R,\omega_R(D_h)\bigr).$$ Here $\omega_R$ is the absolute dualizing sheaf of the reduced simple-normal-crossing divisor $R$. Both forms have weight $a$ and deck character $\chi$.

Multiplication by $\Omega$ after pullback induces a split morphism $$\label{bl:cov:derived-split}
 \mathcal O_E\longrightarrow \mathbf R(q_R)_*\omega_R(D_h)$$ in the derived category of $\mathcal O_E$-modules. Consequently, for every $i\geq0$, it induces an injection $$\label{bl:cov:injections}
 R^ig_*\mathcal O_E\lhook\joinrel\longrightarrow
 R^ih_*\omega_R(D_h),\qquad e'\longmapsto\Omega e'.$$ In particular this holds for $i=1$. The map increases pullback weight by $a$.

Suppose $F_{<0}\mathcal N_0=0$. Then $$\label{bl:hod:symbol-vanishing}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\!\left(H_0,
  \ker\left[F_0\mathcal N_0\otimes T_{T_0}
                \longrightarrow\mathop{\mathrm{gr}}_1^F\mathcal N_0\right]\right)=0.$$ The arrow is the symbol of the right differential-operator action.

For every $j$ there are natural equivariant isomorphisms $$\label{bl:frm:filtered-comparison}
 F_j\mathcal N'
   \simeq \eta^*F_j\mathcal N\otimes\omega_{B'/B}.$$ Under these isomorphisms, symbol action transforms by the tangent map $d\eta:T_{B'}\to\eta^*T_B$.

